    "durationMs": 153505,
    "finalText": "Done. The URL shortener is complete and fully verified against every requirement.\n\n**What I built:**\n- `src/app.js` — Express 5 app with all four endpoints: `POST /links` (201 with generated or custom code, 409 on duplicates, 422 on bad input), `GET /links` (ascending order with `total`), `GET /links/{code}`, and `GET /r/{code}` (atomic `UPDATE clicks = clicks + 1 ... RETURNING url`, so each request increments exactly once, then 302 to the stored URL).\n- **Persistence** — `pg` + `knex` (the idiomatic Node/Postgres stack): `knexfile.js` reads only `DATABASE_URL`, and a knex migration creates the `links` table with a unique `code`, `url` stored exactly as submitted, `clicks`, and a sequential `id` for creation order.\n- `start.sh` — applies pending migrations (`knex migrate:latest`, idempotent) then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`.\n- `README.md` — startup instructions.\n\n**Verified by live HTTP tests:** 201/409/404/422/302 flows, malformed JSON → 422, min/max code lengths, URL validation via Node's standard WHATWG parser (hostless URLs like `http://` correctly rejected), and a full restart cycle where links and click counts survived (clicks continued 1 → 2 across restarts). Test data was cleaned out, and port 8000 is free for the harness to run `sh start.sh`."
  }
